WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:08.340
The mere-exposure effect is a psychological
phenomenon by which we tend to like things

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.380
merely because we are exposed to them.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.460
So, simply exposing you to a random thing
repeatedly, makes you like it.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:23.040
And the same goes for pop songs, products,
and people — with the exception of those

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.780
we dislike initially, seeing them more often
can make us dislike them further.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:35.580
When we plot the mere-exposure effect on a
graph with preferences on one axis and repetitions

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.920
on the other, we will see that it takes about
15 repeats for the effect to reach its full

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:41.520
potential.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.420
If the exposure is too excessive, people get
annoyed.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.680
And if they didn’t like the thing initially,
repeating it can make it worse.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.280
There are 2 main explanations for the phenomenon.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.060
The first is certainty.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.920
We favor what’s familiar.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:04.260
Our minds naturally learned to be careful
around new things that could potentially harm

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:04.440
us.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.960
Which also means that if we see something
repeatedly without bad consequences, we are

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.580
led to believe it is safe.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.020
The Second is perceptual fluency.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:21.420
We are also hardwired to prefer simple things
that take little mental load.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:27.780
Hard cognitive tasks - like figuring out something
new - take lots of attention and time, which

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.340
is why we try to avoid them.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.440
Known for discovering the effect was psychologist
Robert Zajonc, who had noticed that organisms

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.320
exposed to something new, experience fear.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.540
Each subsequent exposure to that new thing
causes less fear and more interest.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:51.840
And with more exposure, the observer begins
to react fondly to the once novel stimulus.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:00.480
Zajonc then tested how subjects responded
to specific symbols, such as Chinese ideograms.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.940
Subjects were shown various characters a different
number of times and were then asked which

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:06.840
ones they liked.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:12.240
Those who were shown certain symbols the most
also rated them as most favorable.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.960
But Zajonc found something even more spectacular.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:24.420
Using a tachistoscope, and a group of people,
he exposed each person to a series of random

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.260
shapes that…

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.520
…changed so quickly that it was impossible
to discern that some were repeated.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:37.260
When the people were later asked which shapes
they found most pleasing, they reliably chose

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.300
those to which they had been exposed the most
often, even though they had no conscious awareness

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:42.800
of that.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.480
The results validated one of Zajonc’s hypotheses:
changes in preferences induced by repeated

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:57.300
exposures depend not on any prior subjective
and conscious evaluations of attributes of

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.280
the stimulus but rather on results from the
objective history of exposures alone.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:10.920
In 1968, advertisers and marketing executives
were able to read Zajonc’s publication.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:16.500
Some were happy to learn that the effect on
the brain can be stronger if exposure comes

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.180
with other pleasant stimuli.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.020
The psychologist continued to study the human
psyche.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.160
Later he famously noted:
For most decisions, it is extremely difficult

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.900
to demonstrate that there has actually been
any prior cognitive process whatsoever.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.940
It isn’t that the decisions people make
are irrational; it’s that the process by

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:44.040
which decisions are made is utterly unlike
the step-by-step rational process that might

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.140
be used to solve, say, a math problem.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:52.560
Decisions are typically made in the unconscious
mind, by means of some unknown process.”

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.960
Now tell us: which of these shapes do you
like the most?

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.100
And were you aware that you were exposed to
one more than the others?

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.020
Also, tell us what you think about ads.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:12.480
If mere exposure to them makes us like things
we otherwise wouldn’t, should billboards

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.280
be banned from the public domain?

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:20.220
The artist Banksy said: Any advert in a public
space that gives you no choice whether you

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.020
see it or not is yours.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:23.340
But is he right?

